AI Snipping Tool Stage 3.2 — private Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 prototype

INSTALLATION (disposable test machine/VM first; not the development host)
The .deb makes no network requests, runs no pip and has no maintainer scripts.
Preinstall its declared Ubuntu dependencies (or provide their full offline bundle).
Then install the local file without downloads, retaining dpkg dependency checks:
  sudo dpkg --install ./ai-snipping-tool_0.3.2~stage3.2-1_amd64.deb
APT --no-download may refuse uncached archives even with local file arguments;
prepare its archive cache explicitly if choosing APT instead. Do not use
--force-depends or system pip to work around missing dependencies.
Missing dependencies must be supplied from a prepared Ubuntu archive first.
This small package does not contain an entire Ubuntu/Qt dependency mirror.
Never run the GUI with sudo. Command: ai-snipping-tool

PER-USER STARTUP (not automatically enabled by package installation)
First inspect existing overrides and launchers:
  systemctl --user show com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.service -p FragmentPath
  systemctl --user cat com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.service
Do not enable if a development/custom ~/.config/systemd/user unit shadows
/usr/lib/systemd/user/com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.service. Preserve it and follow
the reviewed migration instructions in the project packaging report.
On a fresh supported GNOME Wayland user session without an override:
  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user enable --now com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.service
Each account opts in independently. No global enable, lingering or autostart.
The service must have the genuine graphical-session environment, not an
editor's sandbox scope. Log out/in if the user manager environment is stale;
do not guess or import display variables from an editor.

DESKTOP IDENTITY / DATA
Stable ID com.dulmina.aisnippingtool and shortcut ID open_capture_menu retained.
Requested Alt+Shift+S; actual binding is whatever GNOME reports. Confirm portal
permissions interactively if requested; no permission/binding database changes.
Existing ~/.local/share/applications/com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.desktop may shadow
the installed launcher: review/back up explicitly; the package never edits it.
Screenshots remain ~/Pictures/Screenshots. OCR reads Ubuntu eng/sin model data;
no models or screenshots are uploaded or downloaded by the installed build.
The application's existing runtime lock prevents a second capture instance.

UPGRADE / REMOVE / ROLLBACK
Before upgrading/removing in EACH opted-in account:
  systemctl --user disable --now com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.service
Upgrades do not stop existing processes automatically. After a reviewed upgrade:
  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user enable --now com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.service
Removal (after opted-in services are stopped): sudo apt remove ai-snipping-tool
Then systemctl --user daemon-reload. No package scripts delete screenshots,
settings, user service files, launchers or permissions. Other logged-in users
must stop their service too. Do not remove a running multi-user installation.
For rollback, reinstall a retained previous .deb with --no-download, reload and
explicitly activate. For development rollback, stop the packaged service and
restore only reviewed backups of your own previous user unit/launcher; reload
and explicitly restart. Never let both startup mechanisms compete.

ACCEPTANCE
This prototype is not yet clean-machine/physical-package accepted. Check quiet
login startup, correct actual shortcut/overlay/Escape, Rectangle/Window image
paste, English OCR paste, service stop staying stopped and reboot startup.
The application runs without a tray icon/menu or permanent background window.
Use systemctl --user start/stop/restart com.dulmina.aisnippingtool.service for
control; use disable --now to stop and disable future login startup if desired.
Identity and actual shortcut assignment are in the service journal.
No public release; project license remains unspecified by owner's choice.
